archetype (adl_version=1.4; uid=f5fa4407-13db-4262-8a0e-ad52b412f168)
	openEHR-EHR-EVALUATION.pregnancy_care_context.v0

concept
	[at0000]	-- Pregnancy precautions
language
	original_language = <[ISO_639-1::en]>
description
	original_author = <
		["name"] = <"Heather Leslie">
		["organisation"] = <"Atomica Informatics">
		["email"] = <"heather.leslie@atomicainformatics.com">
		["date"] = <"2026-02-23">
	>
	details = <
		["en"] = <
			language = <[ISO_639-1::en]>
			purpose = <"To record the category of pregnancy-related precautions currently applicable to an individual.">
			use = <"Use to record the pregnancy precaution category currently applicable to an individual, to inform their care. Applicability should be assessed from the individual's physiology, without assumptions based on gender identity or sex assigned at birth.

The 'Precaution category' informs, but does not itself represent, subsequent care decisions. Without an explicit category recorded as structured, coded data, pregnancy-related precautions remain implicit in the clinician's reasoning and are unavailable for reliable sharing with other clinicians or use by clinical decision support.

Where pregnancy is physiologically possible and no category has been recorded, 'Routine' should apply by default. An unknown or absent category may prevent pregnancy-related clinical decision support from being applied, creating a potential clinical safety risk. 'Routine' provides a precautionary baseline that can be escalated as clinically necessary, or changed to 'Not applicable' where pregnancy is determined not to be physiologically possible.

'Routine' and 'Elevated' make explicit the precautions that apply when an unrecognised pregnancy is possible, particularly in relation to potential exposure to teratogenic medications, ionising radiation or other potentially harmful interventions. 'Pregnancy' and 'Post pregnancy' make clinically relevant pregnancy or post-pregnancy circumstances explicit, including where these may not otherwise be apparent or known to the current care team. 'Not applicable' makes explicit that pregnancy-related precautions are not relevant to the individual and may be used to avoid unnecessary pregnancy-related questioning or prompts, particularly where repeated questioning may be distressing or traumatic.

The 'Precaution category' should be reassessed as clinical circumstances change and updated where necessary. The current category persists until superseded by a new instance. A new category may be entered directly by a clinician or proposed by a clinical system in response to clinical events, such as menarche, commencing fertility treatment, confirmation of pregnancy, birth or pregnancy loss, completion of post-pregnancy recovery, menopause or hysterectomy. A system-proposed category should be confirmed by a clinician before being recorded.

Some categories, such as 'Elevated' or 'Post pregnancy', may reveal sensitive information about fertility treatment, pregnancy loss or family planning decisions. In some jurisdictions, disclosure of such information may have serious legal consequences for the individual. Access to and sharing of this information should therefore be subject to appropriate privacy, consent and governance controls.

This 'Pregnancy precautions' archetype is intended to be used alongside OBSERVATION.pregnancy_assertion. The 'Precaution category' supports clinical decision-making while carrying a clinically acceptable level of uncertainty about pregnancy. Where a clinical decision requires that uncertainty to be resolved, further investigation can provide the basis for a point-in-time 'Pregnant' or 'Not pregnant' assertion, recorded using OBSERVATION.pregnancy_assertion. Together, these two models separate the distinct clinical meanings often conflated within a single 'Pregnancy status' concept.">
			keywords = <"pregnancy", "precaution", "risk", "postnatal", "postpartum", "antenatal">
			misuse = <"Not to be used to record whether an individual is pregnant at a specific point in time. A 'Not pregnant' determination is valid only at that time and must not be used as an ongoing category. Use the OBSERVATION.pregnancy_assertion archetype for this purpose.

Not to be used to record a suspected pregnancy or the level of diagnostic certainty about pregnancy. Where clinically relevant, a suspected pregnancy may be recorded in the Problem List using EVALUATION.problem_diagnosis with an appropriate level of diagnostic certainty, allowing it to inform clinical decision support until pregnancy is confirmed or excluded.

Not to be used to record summary information about a single pregnancy. Use the EVALUATION.pregnancy_summary archetype for this purpose.

Not to be used to record lactation or breastfeeding status. Use the EVALUATION.breast_feeding_summary archetype for this purpose.

Not to be used to record a precaution about a condition or state of the individual other than pregnancy. Use the EVALUATION.precaution for this purpose.

Not to be used to record the specific care, education or precautions required by an individual during a pregnancy. Use specific archetypes for these purposes.">
			copyright = <"© openEHR Foundation">
		>
	>
	lifecycle_state = <"in_development">
	other_contributors = <"Vebjørn Arntzen, Oslo University Hospital, Norway", "Sparked AU, CSIRO, Australia (openEHR Editor)", "Silje Ljosland Bakke, Helse Vest IKT AS, Norway", "SB Bhattacharyya, Bhattacharyyas Clinical Records Research & Informatics LLP, India", "kim Drever, Kudos Paediatrics, Australia", "William Goossen, Results 4 Care, Netherlands", "Evelyn Hovenga, EJSH Consulting, Australia", "Heather Leslie, Atomica Informatics, Australia (openEHR Editor)", "Ian McNicoll, freshEHR Clinical Informatics, United Kingdom", "Patrick Fergal McSharry, MSMS(I) and SESLHD, Australia", "Martin Milde, Helse Vest IKT, Norway", "Olha Nikolaieva, University Hospital Basel, Switzerland", "Mikael Nyström, Cambio Healthcare Systems AB, Sweden", "Ana Pascual Segura, Catsalut, Spain", "Anton Pichugin, GKU IAC at Department of Information Technologies of the city of Moscow (DIT Moscow), Russia", "Daniel Rodrigues, VirtualCare, Portugal", "Averil Tam, CSIRO, Australia", "Nyree Taylor, CSIRO, Australia (openEHR Editor)">
	other_details = <
		["licence"] = <"This work is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. To view a copy of this license, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/.">
		["custodian_organisation"] = <"openEHR Foundation">
		["original_namespace"] = <"org.openehr">
		["original_publisher"] = <"openEHR Foundation">
		["custodian_namespace"] = <"org.openehr">
		["MD5-CAM-1.0.1"] = <"497CA51837D67F04C72A049E9DAEDAF6">
